\details{
\subsection{Models}{

With \code{model = "cox"}, each landmark gets
\verb{coxph(Surv(.time, .status == cause) ~ <formula>)} with Efron's method for
ties. If the status coding has a single event type, this is an ordinary
Cox model. If it has other event types, they are treated as censored at
their event time, so the model estimates \strong{cause-specific} hazard ratios;
the \code{.model} column then reads \code{"cause-specific cox"}.

With \code{model = "finegray"}, \code{\link[survival:finegray]{survival::finegray()}} expands each landmark's
data with time-dependent censoring weights for \code{cause}, and a weighted
\code{\link[survival:coxph]{survival::coxph()}} is fitted to the expanded data with a robust variance
clustered on subject, as the \code{\link[survival:finegray]{survival::finegray()}} documentation
recommends. Coefficients are log subdistribution hazard ratios. The
censoring distribution is estimated on each landmark's complete-case model
sample and is not stratified; \code{strata()} terms in \code{formula} stratify the
weighted Cox model only.
}

\subsection{Missing covariates and failed fits}{

Each landmark's model uses the rows with no missing values in the variables
of \code{formula}; \code{n_eligible} and \code{n_model} in the results show how many rows
that leaves. A landmark is not fitted (status \code{"unestimable"}) when it has
no subjects at risk, no complete cases, no target events in the model
sample, or a covariate with a single observed value. A fit that warns (for
example about convergence, or a coefficient that may be infinite) gets
status \code{"warning"}, and a fit that errors gets \code{"failed"}. Neither affects
the other landmarks, and one warning at the end lists every landmark that
did not fit cleanly. A Fine-Gray model at a landmark with no competing
events is still fitted (it then equals the Cox model) with status
\code{"warning"}. If a covariate made by an \code{lme} model in
\code{\link[=add_landmark_covariates]{add_landmark_covariates()}} did not fit cleanly at a landmark, that
landmark's status is at least \code{"warning"} and its message names the
covariate.
}

\subsection{Interpretation}{

Each model is conditional on being event-free and under follow-up at its
landmark, so different landmarks describe different populations. Fits at
different landmarks share subjects and are not independent, so differences
between landmarks are not tested. Standard errors treat covariates derived
by \code{\link[=add_landmark_covariates]{add_landmark_covariates()}} as known values; for \code{lme} covariates they
are therefore too small, because the mixed models were estimated from the
same subjects.
